"Internal Error ()\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742 is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/2398/1/A1934-2.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/2398/1/A1934-2.pdf is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www2.gwu.edu/~forcpgm/2025-005.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 52; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www2.gwu.edu/~forcpgm/2025-005.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1726\nL0@P0: Working Papers represent preliminary work circulated for comment and discussion. Please contact the\nL1@P0: author(s) before citing this paper in any publications. The views expressed in Working Papers are solely\nL2@P0: those of the author(s) and do not necessarily represent the views of the H. O. Stekler Research Program on\nL3@P0: Forecasting, the Department of Economics, the Columbian College, or the George Washington University.\nL4@P0: WORKING PAPER SERIES\nL5@P0: FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for\nL6@P0: Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\nL7@P0: Sophia Kazinnik\nL8@P0: Tara M. Sinclair\nL9@P0: Working Paper No. 2025-005\nL10@P0: August, 2025\nL11@P0: H. O. STEKLER RESEARCH PROGRAM ON FORECASTING\nL12@P0: Department of Economics\nL13@P0: Columbian College of Arts & Sciences\nL14@P0: The George Washington University\nL15@P0: Washington, DC 20052\nL16@P0-1: https://cer.columbian.gwu.edu/ho-stekler-research-program-forecastingFOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System\nL17@P1: for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\nL18@P1: This is a preliminary draft. The authors welcome all comments and suggestions.\nL19@P1: Sophia Kazinnik∗ Tara M. Sinclair†\nL20@P1: August 31, 2025\nL21@P1: Abstract\nL22@P1: We develop a multi-agent framework for modeling the Federal Open Market\nL23@P1: Committee (FOMC) decision making process. The framework combines two\nL24@P1: approaches: an LLM-based simulation and a Monte Carlo implementation of\nL25@P1: a generalized Bayesian voting model. Both begin from identical prior beliefs\nL26@P1: about the appropriate interest rate for each committee member, formed using\nL27@P1: real-time data and member profiles. In a simulation replicating the July 2025\nL28@P1: FOMC meeting, both tracks deliver rates near the 4.25–4.50% range’s upper\nL29@P1: end (4.42% LLM, 4.38% MC). Political pressure scenario increases dissent\nL30@P1: and dispersion: the LLM track averages 4.38% and shows dissent in 88% of\nL31@P1: meetings; the MC track averages 4.39% and shows dissent in 61% of meetings.\nL32@P1: A negative jobs revision scenario moves outcomes lower: LLM at 4.30% (dissent\nL33@P1: in 74% of meeting), and MC at 4.32% (dissent in 62% of meeting), with final\nL34@P1: decisions remaining inside the 4.25-4.50% range. The framework isolates small,\nL35@P1: scenario-dependent wedges between behavioral and rational baselines, offering\nL36@P1: an in silico environment for counterfactual evaluation in monetary policy.\nL37@P1: Keywords: Generative AI; Multi-Agent Systems; Large Language Models,\nL38@P1: Federal Open Market Committee; Monetary Policy; Simulations\nL39@P1: JEL Codes: E52, E58, C63, D83, C73\nL40@P1: ∗Email: kazinnik@stanford.edu.\nL41@P1: †Email: tsinc@gwu.edu.\nL42@P1: We thank Erik Brynjolfsson, Thomas R. Cook, Seung Jung Lee, Andrew Martinez, and Daniela\nL43@P1: Puzzello, as well as the participants at the DEL brownbag series. We are grateful to the Stanford\nL44@P1: Digital Economy Lab for funding and support. All remaining errors are our own.\nL45@P1-2: 11 Introduction\nL46@P2: “Between the idea and the reality . . .\nL47@P2: falls the shadow.”\nL48@P2: T. S. Eliot, “The Hollow Men,”\nL49@P2: Monetary policy deliberations and the actual rate decision are separated by a\nL50@P2: “shadow” of persuasion and norms. Decisions by the Federal Open Market Committee\nL51@P2: (FOMC) reflect more than macroeconomic data and formal models; they also depend\nL52@P2: on members’ experience, behavioral biases, and implicit rules (e.g., Hughes and\nL53@P2: Fehrler, 2015; Haldane, 2018; Maih et al., 2021). Consequently, who participates in\nL54@P2: the deliberation and how information is communicated can influence policy outcomes\nL55@P2: beyond the underlying economic data.\nL56@P2: Precisely because of these behavioral forces, traditional models of committee\nL57@P2: decision making, though useful and mathematically elegant, often miss the complex\u0002ities of real world interactions. Behavioral economics highlights how biases, well\nL58@P2: documented by Tversky and Kahneman (1974) and shown to affect monetary policy\nL59@P2: (Haldane, 2018), can systematically shape decisions. Information design matters too:\nL60@P2: Bayesian persuasion demonstrates how agenda setters steer beliefs (Kamenica and\nL61@P2: Gentzkow, 2011). Separately, robust control preferences reveal how officials facing\nL62@P2: model uncertainty skew choices toward caution (Hansen and Sargent, 2008). Within\nL63@P2: the FOMC, policy outcomes depend on the committee’s composition, from regional\nL64@P2: voting rotation (e.g., Fos and Xu, 2025) to individual members’ policy leanings (e.g.,\nL65@P2: Hack et al., 2023).\nL66@P2: To better capture such frictions, researchers increasingly use computational\nL67@P2: approaches, such as agent-based models (Dwarakanath et al., 2024) and reinforcement\nL68@P2: learning (Hinterlang and Tänzer, 2021). Large language models (LLMs) further\nL69@P2: extend the frontier by enabling agents with coherent communication and emergent\nL70@P2: behavior. LLM-based generative agents simulate realistic social interactions (Park\nL71@P2: et al., 2022) and market dynamics (Zhang et al., 2024), opening new possibilities for\nL72@P2: policy experimentation (Argyle et al., 2022; Horton, 2023).\nL73@P2: However, no existing approach lets us observe rational and behavioral decision\nL74@P2-3: 2rules on the same information set. We address this gap with a dual track simulation\nL75@P3: framework that integrates an LLM-driven simulation of the FOMC meeting with a\nL76@P3: game theory voting model. In the rational (game theory) track, policymakers observe\nL77@P3: macro data, update beliefs via Bayesian inference, and vote to an equilibrium. In the\nL78@P3: behavioral (LLM) track, the agents get the same data, reason in natural language,\nL79@P3: engage in committee debate, and generate outcomes shaped not only by the data but\nL80@P3: also by persona heterogeneity and institutional norms that formal models struggle to\nL81@P3: capture. Recent studies show that LLM-based agents can reproduce various aspects\nL82@P3: of market dynamics (e.g., Kazinnik, 2023; Manning et al., 2024; Lopez-Lira, 2025);\nL83@P3: we apply the same logic to monetary policy. We generalize the Riboni and Ruge\u0002Murcia (2010) framework to admit heterogeneous preferences, strategic information\nL84@P3: disclosure, and a political pressure channel. Deviations between the LLM equilibrium\nL85@P3: and the analytic benchmark then isolate the behavioral frictions that standard models\nL86@P3: omit.\nL87@P3: Specifically, we construct and run two types of simulations: a set of LLM\u0002driven deliberations and a parallel Monte Carlo (MC) analysis. Both start from\nL88@P3: the same LLM-generated set of heterogeneous private opinions, reflecting FOMC\nL89@P3: members’ profiles and the current economic state. The MC simulation serves as\nL90@P3: a statistical baseline, modeling thousands of voting scenarios under theoretical\nL91@P3: framework described in Riboni and Ruge-Murcia (2010). The LLM simulation, by\nL92@P3: contrast, introduces deliberation, persuasion, and strategic interaction, capturing\nL93@P3: human-like dynamics. Comparing the two allows us to isolate and measure the\nL94@P3: impact of deliberation by identifying where LLM-driven outcomes diverge from the\nL95@P3: statistical baseline.\nL96@P3: Within the framework, we start by automatically ingesting real-time macroe\u0002conomic data, Governor speeches, district-level reports, and financial news. We\nL97@P3: then build detailed profiles for each committee member, combining their historical\nL98@P3: policy stances, biographies, recent speeches, district conditions, and the current\nL99@P3: macroeconomic context.1 Each agent draws on this information to generate an initial\nL100@P3: 1The framework supports introducing new committee members, alternative economic conditions,\nL101@P3: and other counterfactual scenarios.\nL102@P3-4: 3rate recommendation, comprising a preferred policy rate, a confidence score, and a\nL103@P4: short explanation, capturing the agent’s initial belief.\nL104@P4: With these agent profiles and initial beliefs in place, we launch the simulation\nL105@P4: to model the decision-making process under two approaches. The LLM simulation\nL106@P4: begins with several optional rounds of informal coordination to align member views.2\nL107@P4: The simulation then enters a deliberation stage, where a discussion engine records\nL108@P4: arguments and updates members’ initial beliefs, and concludes with a structured\nL109@P4: voting phase in which members submit final rate preferences for automatic aggregation\nL110@P4: and comparison. Outputs include posterior beliefs, vote patterns with dissent\nL111@P4: diagnostics, and full deliberation logs. The MC framework uses the same priors\nL112@P4: and economic parameters but removes conversational and behavioral dynamics,\nL113@P4: running thousands of rule-based voting scenarios to generate a statistical baseline.\nL114@P4: We benchmark the LLM results against MC ones.\nL115@P4: To showcase the framework, we simulate two scenarios. The first one introduces\nL116@P4: the effects of a politically charged environment on the committee. This treatment\nL117@P4: contains two distinct but related elements, designed to model both the direct pressure\nL118@P4: on the current Chair and the indirect, forward-looking behavior of the other committee\nL119@P4: members. The first component targets the Chair directly: we apply a dovish bias\nL120@P4: in the Chair’s agenda proposal. Simultaneously, the Chair’s institutional authority\nL121@P4: is diminished by removing the extra voting weight their ballot typically carries.\nL122@P4: The second component of the treatment models the strategic, forward looking\nL123@P4: behavior of the other committee members who anticipate an imminent regime change.\nL124@P4: Anticipating a more dovishly inclined successor3, members face asymmetric career\nL125@P4: incentives, so dissent costs are direction-dependent: hawkish votes carry a higher\nL126@P4: penalty, while dovish votes are cheaper, signaling alignment with the expected\nL127@P4: regime.4\nL128@P4: The second scenario examines the consequences of a sudden negative labor market\nL129@P4: data revision. This treatment feeds into the simulation as a downward adjustment\nL130@P4: 2The Monte Carlo (MC) implements the same optional mechanism.\nL131@P4: 3Needs explanation or is clear\nL132@P4: 4\nL133@P4: In the current FOMC roster, the potential candidates are Waller (90%) and Bowman (10%),\nL134@P4: reflecting speculative assessments under a dovish administration based on background, policy views,\nL135@P4: and feasibility.\nL136@P4-5: 4to the perceived strength of payroll growth, paired with an increase in the assessed\nL137@P5: uncertainty of employment sensitive members. By modeling this as a real-time\nL138@P5: information shock, we capture how the timing and nature of data updates can\nL139@P5: meaningfully reshape deliberation and decision making. In our framework, when\nL140@P5: members receive new information plays a critical role in shaping the path of discussion,\nL141@P5: belief updating, and ultimately, the policy vote.\nL142@P5: Our findings are as follows. In the baseline LLM scenario, simulating economic\nL143@P5: conditions as of late July 2025 (when the target FFR stood at 4.25%–4.50%, and\nL144@P5: effective FFR stood at 4.33%), the committee starts off with a moderately hawkish\nL145@P5: tilt, with individual rate preferences spanning 4.20% (for simulated Christopher\nL146@P5: Waller, or simWaller) to 4.62% (for simulated Alberto Musalem, or simMusalem)\nL147@P5: and clustering at the upper end of the range.5 The average initial recommendation\nL148@P5: across members is at 4.375%, with a belief spread of just over 55 basis points. As\nL149@P5: deliberations unfold, hawks emphasize the persistence of above-target inflation.6\nL150@P5: The committee converges unanimously on a final rate of 4.42%, implying an upward\nL151@P5: drift of roughly 5 basis points in the midpoint federal funds rate (4.375%) without\nL152@P5: changing the 4.25%–4.50% range. Parallel Monte Carlo simulations, which remove\nL153@P5: conversational dynamics, also converge near the existing range but yield a slightly\nL154@P5: lower mean of 4.376%. Outcomes cluster tightly and no dissents occur. Debate thus\nL155@P5: introduces a modest hawkish bias of around 5 basis points, relative to rule-based\nL156@P5: voting.\nL157@P5: Next, political pressure scenario (mirroring public attacks by the POTUS on Chair\nL158@P5: Powell) reduces the Chair’s agenda weight, applies a dovish bias to his proposal, and\nL159@P5: imposes career-driven dovish shifts for members with high appointment probabilities\nL160@P5: (simWaller, and, to lesser extent simBowman). Member views polarize: simWaller’s\nL161@P5: 5The range midpoint is 4.375%; 7 of 12 members are above it and the mean (4.39%) is slightly\nL162@P5: higher.\nL163@P5: 6For example, in one simulation, simWaller warns that ‘..if we allow inflation\nL164@P5: to spiral further, it may lead to more significant economic instability,” while sim\u0002ulated simSchmid stresses that “allowing inflation to persist unchecked can have\nL165@P5: long-term detrimental effects on both employment and economic growth,” particularly\nL166@P5: if expectations become “unanchored.” By the final discussion round, the effect of these argu\u0002ments is visible: simPowell shifts position, noting that “the current inflation data cannot\nL167@P5: be overlooked” and proposing a move to 4.50%. See Section 3.2 for background on belief formation\nL168@P5: and debate mechanics.\nL169@P5-6: 5preferred rate drops to an average of 3.97%, and he dissents in 88% of simulations.\n"
